  message: "Closed wont_do, owner-approved 2026-10-03: the question was already ruled before this item was filed.\n- WI-pavob, owner ruling 2026-08-26 (verbatim \"yes in\"): A CLOCK READ IS host_info_read. Reason: fingerprinting via clock skew, and CLOCK_MONOTONIC leaks uptime.\n- WI-tubij (PR #579, c6def4667c, 2026-08-27) shipped clock rows as host_info_read in every language. The registry definition (io_boundary_types.py ~249) says \"INCLUDING THE CLOCK (WI-pavob)\" and that its low discriminating power \"was accepted on the record\".\n- This item (filed 2026-09-07) cites neither. Its premise, \"a monotonic counter identifies nothing about the host\", is the argument the ruling rejected; java.yaml's currentTimeMillis/nanoTime row note gives the reason.\n- Its noise claim is overstated. Measurement 0017 attributes the 5 jenkins clock findings to flow imprecision (no value reaches the sink), not to the boundary: the same residual appears with System.getenv as the source. On cassandra, 0 verdicts moved. Java has had a DDG since WI-gotun (#1245), so the 09-07 figures are stale.\n- The question that remains, whether a claim can exclude the ubiquitous clock reads (or whether host_info_read should mint a taint source at all), is WI-tilig's. It is not pursued here."  # b1f7
